WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.890
If something broke down at midnight, how many numbers in your phone could you call without embarrassment?

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:11.620
Your contact list holds a thousand people, but you stop when the list narrows to five names.

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:19.110
Aristotle said why two thousand years ago: some friendships exist not because we want them, but because they are useful to us.

4
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.160
You do someone a kindness.

5
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:36.850
You find them a job, you help them move house, you pick up the phone on a hard day and listen for hours.

6
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.660
They thank you, and it is a sincere thank-you at that.

7
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:49.910
And right at that moment, a question you never invited crosses a corner of your mind: if I had nothing left to give, would this person still call?

8
00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.440
The question feels shameful.

9
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.090
As though you were smuggling an accounting ledger into a friendship.

10
00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:01.820
And yet the most serious thinkers in human history did not flinch from asking exactly this.

11
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.390
The oldest and most systematic answer we have comes to us from 2400 years ago.

12
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.480
Aristotle divided his work the Nicomachean Ethics into ten books.

13
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.490
Two of those ten, the eighth and the ninth, he gave over from beginning to end to a single subject: friendship.

14
00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:26.660
In the densest text of moral philosophy, the fact that he grants friendship more room than he grants justice is no accident.

15
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:33.670
He puts it this way: even if a person had everything else, without friends he would not choose to live.

16
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.320
And he adds something still more striking.

17
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:46.090
Lawgivers, he says, seem to care more about friendship than about justice; because if people are friends to one another, they have no further need of justice.

18
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.420
Aristotle begins by drawing a distinction.

19
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:56.910
Friendship is not one thing, he says; we give the same name to three things that resemble one another without being the same.

20
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.760
The first is the friendship of utility.

21
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.090
You love the other person for the benefit they bring you.

22
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:08.180
The neighbour you discuss the parking problem with because you live on the same street.

23
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:13.590
The colleague who leaks you information before a meeting, and to whom you leak information in return.

24
00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.520
The acquaintance who gives you a reference when you are job-hunting and then asks you for one.

25
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.330
These are real relationships, not counterfeit ones.

26
00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.620
But what holds them upright is the exchange running between the two of you.

27
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.630
The second is the friendship of pleasure.

28
00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:33.440
You love the other person because you have a good time when they are near.

29
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:41.730
Their jokes are funny, you support the same team, you listen to the same music, at three in the morning you can still find something to talk about.

30
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.740
This too is a real bond.

31
00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:49.030
But what holds it upright is the enjoyment running between the two of you.

32
00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.320
There is no contempt in the way Aristotle looks at these two kinds.

33
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:58.890
Most human relationships are like this, and they have to be; this is how life runs.

34
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:05.100
But he makes a diagnosis: in these two friendships, the person is not loved for being who they are.

35
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.750
They are loved for a property that happens to attach to them.

36
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:15.120
When the usefulness is cut off, when the pleasure runs out, the ground the affection stood on disappears.

37
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.410
That is why such bonds are easily formed and easily dissolved.

38
00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.580
You do not need to read philosophy to test this.

39
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.750
Look at what happens when you change jobs.

40
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:34.520
The group chat you set up with people you had lunch with every day for years, whose birthdays you knew, whose homes you visited, falls silent within three weeks.

41
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.210
Nobody is angry with anybody.

42
00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.060
Nobody has betrayed anybody.

43
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:48.990
It is only that the ground holding the bond upright has been pulled away: being in the same building, sharing the same troubles, complaining about the same person.

44
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:52.160
When the ground goes, the structure goes with it.

45
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.130
Aristotle also separates the young from the old here.

46
00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:03.620
The old form friendships of utility more often, he says, because they are looking not for what is pleasant but for what is useful.

47
00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:09.990
The young form friendships of pleasure; they live by feeling and chase whatever gives them delight in the moment.

48
00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:16.360
That is why the friendships of the young flare up quickly and burn out quickly; they begin in a day and end in a day.

49
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.250
And the third?

50
00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.380
The third is what Aristotle calls friendship in the full sense of the word.

51
00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.470
Here you love the other person not for some property attached to them, but for what they are.

52
00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:33.360
You want what is good for them not because it touches your own interest, but because it is good for them.

53
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.650
When they are promoted, not one bitter vein stirs inside you.

54
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:43.860
When they move far away, you grieve even though you know perfectly well that you will never receive anything from them again.

55
00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.670
Aristotle says openly that this is rare.

56
00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:53.880
And the reason it is rare is not human wickedness but the price this friendship exacts: time.

57
00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:01.530
He reminds the reader of a proverb; two people cannot know one another until they have consumed together enough salt to establish a claim between them.

58
00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:08.380
In Turkish there is a phrase for this too, the claim of salt and bread, and it says exactly the same thing.

59
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:13.150
Meeting someone happens in an evening; knowing them takes years.

60
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:19.040
Now let us return to the question we began with, because in answering it Aristotle ties a knot.

61
00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.810
Friendship in the full sense is not a friendship without benefit.

62
00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:29.060
On the contrary, it is the most beneficial of all; it is also the most pleasant.

63
00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:34.470
A good friend is useful to you, and you have a good time in their company.

64
00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:40.200
The difference lies not in whether benefit is present, but in where the benefit stands.

65
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:46.250
In a friendship of utility, advantage is the foundation; pull it out and the building collapses.

66
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:52.780
In a friendship of character, advantage is a fruit hanging from a branch; pick it and the tree stays where it is.

67
00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.470
So the question changes shape.

68
00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:00.080
The real issue is not whether friendship without self-interest can exist.

69
00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.490
The real issue is this: do I love this person independently of what they give me?

70
00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.500
And how am I supposed to know?

71
00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:15.350
In a text belonging to the Aristotelian tradition, there is a strange and unforgettable comparison.

72
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:22.040
When we want to see our own face, we look into a mirror; when we want to know ourselves, we look at a friend.

73
00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:26.330
The first time you hear it, the comparison sounds like a pleasant saying.

74
00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.460
But when you dwell on it, a serious claim emerges from underneath.

75
00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.750
The claim is this: a person cannot see themselves directly.

76
00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:38.560
Everyone has experienced the most concrete version of this.

77
00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.170
When you hear your own voice on a recording, you do not recognise it.

78
00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:48.580
The voice seems not to belong to you, and it is faintly disturbing.

79
00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:54.470
This is because while you speak you hear your voice through the bones of your skull; other people hear it through the air.

80
00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:59.880
The voice you have been hearing for years is a voice nobody but you has ever heard.

81
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.570
Character works the same way.

82
00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:05.420
You hear yourself from the inside.

83
00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.670
You know your intentions, you know your reasons, you know how tired you were that day.

84
00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:14.640
That is why you always read your own behaviour in the best light.

85
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:21.010
If someone else did the same thing, you would call it selfishness; when you do it, you say it was a difficult day.

86
00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:27.220
We are not impartial when we judge ourselves, because we are both the judge and the defendant in the case.

87
00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.230
This is where the friend's mirror function comes in.

88
00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:35.640
A friend is the only eye that sees you from the outside without being hostile to you.

89
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:46.810
Your enemy also sees you from the outside, and in fact sees some of your faults more clearly than anyone; but when they say so, you do not believe them, because there is an intent to wound in the way they say it.

90
00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:52.700
A stranger sees but does not speak, because it is no concern of theirs.

91
00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:58.750
Only a friend both sees and speaks, and in speaking wants not to demolish you but to correct you.

92
00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:03.040
This is precisely why Aristotle calls a friend another self.

93
00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.010
That is not a romantic phrase but a technical definition.

94
00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.460
A friend is the place from which I am able to look at myself from outside.

95
00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:18.310
But right here comes the sharpest objection the idea faces, and it is an objection people still make constantly today.

96
00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:24.200
If a friend is another self, then loving them is still loving myself, isn't it?

97
00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:32.810
In that case there is no such thing as friendship without self-interest; there is only a more refined, more polite form of self-interest.

98
00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:37.100
I do good things for them because doing good makes me feel good.

99
00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:41.710
I rejoice in their happiness because rejoicing pleases me.

100
00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.080
In the end it is always me.

101
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.330
Aristotle saw this objection, and he answered it by dividing self-love in two.

102
00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:59.700
There is one kind of self-love that pushes a person toward money, rank and bodily pleasure; it always wants to reserve more for itself, and in doing so takes from someone else.

103
00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.190
This is the selfishness everybody condemns.

104
00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:12.280
But there is another kind of self-love: a person assigning to themselves what is best and most noble; wanting to be someone they will not be ashamed of.

105
00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:21.050
According to Aristotle, the good person loves themselves in this second sense, and it is exactly for this reason that they can sacrifice for a friend.

106
00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:26.140
Because for them the real gain is not the thing held onto, but the person they become.

107
00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:33.150
So the sentence "I am doing it for myself" is not as devastating as the objector supposes.

108
00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.320
The question is how far that "myself" extends.

109
00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.050
In a friendship of utility, I end where my skin ends, and the other person is a resource.

110
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:48.100
In a friendship of character, my boundary has shifted; what happens to them has happened to me.

111
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:52.710
This is not the settling of the account, but the disappearance of the ledger.

112
00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:59.080
The French thinker Montaigne is perhaps the person who described this experience most plainly.

113
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:10.410
His friendship with Étienne de La Boétie, whom he placed at the centre of his life, lasted only a few years; La Boétie died in 1563, at the age of 32.

114
00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:19.820
In his Essays, Montaigne tries to explain why he loved this bond so much, searches for a reason, cannot find one, and in the end gives up.

115
00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:25.710
The only thing he can say is this: because it was him, because it was me.

116
00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.760
There is no reason.

117
00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.730
If there were a reason, the friendship would end when the reason ended.

118
00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:36.340
And once you set the bar this high, how many people are left?

119
00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:39.510
Here Aristotle is mercilessly realistic.

120
00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:47.480
Friendship in the full sense cannot be formed with many people, he says; just as a person cannot be passionately in love with many people at once.

121
00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:54.970
A bond of this kind demands time, demands attention, demands a life lived together; and all of these are limited resources.

122
00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:58.620
And there is a saying attributed to him in his old age.

123
00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:08.030
Reported by the ancient biographer Diogenes Laertius, and still argued over today as to how the text should even be read, the sentence runs: "O my friends,

124
00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:15.840
there is no friend." Said to have come from the mouth of a man who wrote two whole books on friendship, this is perhaps not a denial;

125
00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.570
it is the confession of someone who set the standard so high that he struggled to find a person who met it.

126
00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:31.460
A version of that same confession would step onto the stage some 1400 years after Aristotle, in an entirely different part of the world, in the life of an entirely different man.

127
00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.310
The year is 1095.

128
00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:35.880
Baghdad.

129
00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:45.770
The head teacher of the most prestigious educational institution of the age, the Nizamiyya madrasa, is a man not yet forty: Abu Hamid al-Ghazali.

130
00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:49.580
He stands at the summit of the scholarly world of his time.

131
00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:56.110
He has hundreds of students, a relationship with the court, and a name known for being unbeatable in debate.

132
00:11:56.110 --> 00:12:00.880
And one day he stands up in the lecture hall and cannot speak.

133
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.410
His tongue seizes.

134
00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:09.460
Physicians are called; they find nothing wrong with his body and say the illness comes from the heart.

135
00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:18.070
In the account of his own life that he wrote afterwards, usually rendered in English as Deliverance from Error, al-Ghazali describes this crisis in detail:

136
00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:28.280
he has reached the point where he can no longer be certain that he truly knows the things he has been teaching for years, nor even whether he has been teaching them for the sake of God or for the sake of fame.

137
00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.970
In the end he leaves everything.

138
00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:40.060
He gives up his chair, his salary, his title; he goes to Damascus, to Jerusalem, from there on pilgrimage, and remains alone for years.

139
00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:46.590
His masterwork, the Revival of the Religious Sciences, is largely the product of these years of withdrawal.

140
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:49.120
Now here is the real question.

141
00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.370
When Baghdad's most powerful teacher left his chair, what remained of the crowd around him?

142
00:12:54.370 --> 00:13:02.500
Al-Ghazali does not ask this directly, but inside the Revival he places an entire book devoted to the etiquette of companionship and friendship.

143
00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:08.390
And there he gives an extremely practical, almost cool-headed list of who should be taken as a friend.

144
00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:13.320
According to al-Ghazali, five qualities are sought in a person you would befriend.

145
00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:18.890
They must be intelligent, because friendship with a fool brings harm even when the fool means well.

146
00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:25.900
They must be of good character, because a person who is overcome by their anger burns the friendship down the moment they get angry.

147
00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:30.990
They must not be dissolute, that is, must not have made sin a habit.

148
00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.320
They must not be a person of religious innovation.

149
00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.810
And they must not be attached to the world.

150
00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.660
This fifth one is the heart of the list.

151
00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:48.150
A person greedily bound to the world sees friendship as a kind of rank too; they keep it while it pays and drop it when it costs.

152
00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:54.520
Al-Ghazali describes attachment to the world as a lethal poison, because it is contagious.

153
00:13:54.520 --> 00:14:02.010
Stand near such a person long enough, and you too will begin to see what people are good for before you see anything else about them.

154
00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:08.220
Against this, al-Ghazali says that friendship is not a dry act of selection but a chain of obligations.

155
00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:18.590
A friend has rights over a friend: a share of one's property, bodily help in a moment of need, a tongue that will defend them in their absence, an eye that sees their fault and covers it,

156
00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:25.600
prayer said for them behind their back, and a loyalty that continues toward their family even after their death.

157
00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.890
Notice that none of these rights is made conditional on reciprocation.

158
00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:35.940
Especially the last one: looking after the children of a person who can never give you anything again.

159
00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.070
This is the point where self-interest is impossible by definition.

160
00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:48.040
This understanding had an institution in Anatolia, and that institution turned friendship from a feeling into a craft: the Akhi brotherhood.

161
00:14:48.040 --> 00:15:00.170
In the thirteenth century, in the years when the Mongol invasion was turning Anatolia upside down, Shaykh Nasir al-Din Mahmud, known as Ahi Evran, gathered the tradesmen of Kayseri and then of Kırşehir,

162
00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:05.420
especially the tanners, the leather workers, into an organisation.

163
00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:15.470
The name of this organisation was futuwwa; the word comes from a root meaning youth and bravery, and rests on a moral tradition carried from Baghdad to Anatolia.

164
00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:23.760
As for the word "akhi", some researchers say it comes from the Arabic word meaning "my brother", others from an old Turkish word meaning "generous".

165
00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.130
Both describe the same thing.

166
00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:37.780
In the futuwwa charters, the written statutes of the Akhi brotherhood, appears the rule everybody knows by heart: you shall keep three things bound, your hand, your tongue and your loins;

167
00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:42.710
and you shall keep three things open, your table, your door and your heart.

168
00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:48.440
To understand what this rule actually amounted to, it is worth listening to an outside eye.

169
00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:55.450
The Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta wandered through Anatolia in the early fourteenth century and could not believe what he saw.

170
00:15:55.450 --> 00:16:02.620
In every city he entered, young men he had never met competed with one another for the right to host him and his companions.

171
00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:11.870
According to his account in his Travels, the competition reached such a pitch that the young men would argue over which lodge would receive the guest, and it nearly came to blows.

172
00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:20.000
These people worked during the day, laid out what they had earned in the evening, and passed the night in food, conversation and sema.

173
00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:25.570
This has to be seen clearly: to them, Ibn Battuta was a completely useless human being.

174
00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:29.860
He was not their customer, not their neighbour, not their relative.

175
00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:34.310
He would leave tomorrow, never return, and owe nothing to anyone.

176
00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:39.560
The attention shown to him was pure precisely because any return was impossible.

177
00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:44.170
But here one has to be honest, because the sorrow lies exactly there.

178
00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:48.300
The Akhi brotherhood was not a pure friendship club.

179
00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:58.030
It was at the same time a professional organisation; it set prices, inspected quality, bound the apprentice to the master, and turned the journeyman into a shop owner.

180
00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:01.840
In other words, there was self-interest in it, plenty of it.

181
00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:07.890
What they did was not to pretend self-interest away; it was to place self-interest underneath friendship.

182
00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:12.180
There was profit, but morality decided how the profit would be shared.

183
00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:18.550
The economy was built inside friendship, rather than friendship inside the economy, as it is today.

184
00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:22.040
The sorrow is that this frame fell apart.

185
00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:29.690
The guilds were liquidated in the nineteenth century, the lodges were closed, the apprentice no longer grew up in the master's house.

186
00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.820
What remained was longing, but no structure left to carry the longing.

187
00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:39.550
Today the person who sighs that friendships used to be different usually thinks that people have changed.

188
00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:46.240
It is not people who changed; it is the order that used to bring them together in the same courtyard six days a week.

189
00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.930
And what took the place of that order?

190
00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:53.220
This, at last, we can measure.

191
00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:57.030
Today we can count how many friends a person has.

192
00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:02.600
The number is written at the top of the screen: followers, connections, friends.

193
00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:06.410
And that number is larger than it has ever been in human history.

194
00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:15.020
In the 1990s, the British anthropologist Robin Dunbar made an estimate based on the relationship between the size of primate groups and brain structure.

195
00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:20.270
The number of relationships a human being can stably sustain is around 150.

196
00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:25.040
But the truly interesting part is the layers inside that 150.

197
00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:36.530
Furthest out, the crowd whose faces and names you know; inside that, the 50 people you see occasionally; further in, the 15 who would genuinely be upset by your troubles; and at the very centre,

198
00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.700
the 5 you could call at four in the morning.

199
00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:48.630
Dunbar's warning is this: social networks expanded the outermost ring without limit, but did not enlarge that central ring of 5 by a single millimetre.

200
00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:55.000
Because what enlarges that ring is not information but time spent together, and time does not stretch.

201
00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:01.210
Your follower count can be in the tens of thousands while the number of people who know you is still in single digits.

202
00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.900
The cost of this is not abstract.

203
00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:12.830
In 2023, Vivek Murthy, then the Surgeon General of the United States, issued an official advisory on loneliness and social disconnection.

204
00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:23.840
The report's most widely discussed finding was this: lacking sufficient social connection raises the risk of early death by an amount comparable to the harm of smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day.

205
00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:29.090
Loneliness is now discussed not merely as a mood but as a public health matter.

206
00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:37.220
On the other hand, there is a study showing the positive side of the same equation, probably the longest-running research project in the world.

207
00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:50.150
The Study of Adult Development, begun at Harvard University in 1938, followed hundreds of young men across their entire lives; the research continues today, and now covers the participants' children as well.

208
00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:58.920
Out of decades of accumulated health records, income data and cholesterol readings, the study's director Robert Waldinger sums up the result this way:

209
00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:07.210
the best predictor of health and happiness at 80 was not the cholesterol level at 50, but satisfaction with one's relationships at 50.

210
00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:11.340
So friendship without self-interest is not a luxury ornament.

211
00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:15.150
It is part of the infrastructure that lengthens a human life.

212
00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:17.520
And what are we to do with this knowledge?

213
00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:25.970
Aristotle's standard is very high, al-Ghazali's list is very exacting, and the order of the Akhi brotherhood passed into history long ago.

214
00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:28.820
What do we actually have in hand?

215
00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:32.630
In fact we have something quite clear: a direction.

216
00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:36.600
First, friendship is not an adjective but a verb.

217
00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:41.370
The sentence "we are friends" states a condition and costs nothing.

218
00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:44.220
Friendship is what gets done.

219
00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:55.230
Aristotle's observation on this is still jolting: friendship lies in loving rather than in being loved; those who are praised are not the ones who are loved, but the ones who love their friends.

220
00:20:55.230 --> 00:21:00.960
In other words, the measure of the bond is not how much you are valued, but how much you give.

221
00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:05.730
This means the only real point of control in the relationship belongs to you.

222
00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:10.980
Second, the only sound test is time, because time cannot be faked.

223
00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:17.830
Words can be imitated, gifts can be bought, interest can be performed; but three hours are three hours.

224
00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:22.440
Look at who gives you their time; and look at which time they give.

225
00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:28.010
Are they handing you the leftovers of their free evening, or are they coming by giving something else up?

226
00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:33.740
Third, the real test of disinterestedness is not the crisis but the ordinary stretch.

227
00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:39.950
In a crisis people gather; coming to a funeral is easy, because it is defined and brief.

228
00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:45.840
What really distinguishes people is whose mind you cross on a Tuesday evening when nothing at all is happening.

229
00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:51.090
Self-interest is impatient; it likes events, not duration.

230
00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:58.260
Fourth, and hardest of all: this has less to do with finding such a friendship than with becoming capable of it.

231
00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:09.110
For Aristotle, a friendship of character can only be formed between good people who resemble one another; which is to say that if you are looking for someone who will love without expecting a return,

232
00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:13.560
you first have to become someone who can love without expecting a return.

233
00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:20.410
Al-Ghazali's list, too, is as much a checklist for self-examination as it is a guide for searching.

234
00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:27.260
If you want a friend who is not attached to the world, pay attention to what is the first thing that comes to your mind when you look at people.

235
00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.910
And finally, this has to be admitted.

236
00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:36.480
Friendship without self-interest is possible, but it is not free.

237
00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:42.850
On the contrary, it is paid for with the most expensive thing a person owns: time that never comes back.

238
00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:47.460
You cannot use again the evening you gave to someone.

239
00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:52.550
You can neither buy it, nor borrow it, nor make it up afterwards.

240
00:22:52.550 --> 00:23:01.320
This is why time is the currency of friendship least open to forgery; and this is why real friendship is disinterested not because you expect nothing while giving,

241
00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:05.450
but because what you gave has become too much a part of you to be asked back.

242
00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:09.260
You may have a thousand followers.

243
00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:18.350
The question is whether there is, on that list, a number you could dial at two in the morning; and, more importantly, whether you are that number on somebody else's list.

244
00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:22.320
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

245
00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:26.130
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
